Hi all,

I`m struggling with the same problem. My survey area differs across site and occasion. Its a long-term monitoring data and the transect area was not fixed at the beginning of the monitoring.

I tried this:

N[i,t] ~ dpois(lambda[i,t])
    log(lambda[i,t]) <- alpha.lam[t] + alpha.lam2*log(Area[i,t])+ eps[i]

but got the error
RUNTIME ERROR:
Unable to resolve the following parameters:
Area[1,1].....

Is it a big problem to use the count data standardized by the same transect area until I find a better solution?

Thanks!

Em terça-feira, 14 de julho de 2020 15:15:17 UTC-3, Mathias Tobler escreveu:

> Hi Marc and Jerry,
>
> Jerry send some simple simulation code and it looks like the model I sent is not estimable without additional information that helps estimate detection probability (double observer, distance). My intuition was the same as Marc's, it should work if density is assumed constant, but apparently it does not, or at least not in that form. The situation is easier if survey effort varies across sites but is constant across surveys within sites which is described in the HM book 6.9 for constant areas but varying transect length or can be modeled with a simple offset if survey area differs. I will keep looking into this a bit more since I have a similar dataset where survey effort varies across survey occasions (I did not collect the data).
>
> Best,
>
> Mathias
>
>
> On 14.07.2020 10:46, Jerry Cole wrote:

> > Thanks for the thoughts Mathias and Marc. I'll have to think more about whether it's reasonable to assume that density remains constant even when tidal conditions change. I appreciate everyone's responses both on and off list.
> >
> > On Tuesday, July 14, 2020 at 2:50:04 AM UTC-7 marc.kery wrote:
> > > Sorry, Mathias, was working through emails in chronological order after coming back from vacations. You said it all already.
